          "[E10]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 121,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "The hopes for a breakthrough, however, were soon dashed, since Zalachenko had never operated in Sweden and had little knowledge of the country. On the other hand, Zalachenko had heard the rumour of a “Red Jumper,” a highly placed Swede—or possibly other Scandinavian politician—who worked for the KGB.\nGullberg drew up a list of names that were connected to Palme: Carl Lidbom, Pierre Schori, Sten Andersson, Marita Ulvskog, and a number of others. For the rest of his life, Gullberg would come back again and again to that list, but he never found an answer.",
            "paragraph_end": 122,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "25ac84fab773ba1d2c350a98656141fc6d96a8342af6fbc8a9843bdb53daf22b",
            "id": "25ac84fa-b773-ba1d-2c35-0a98656141fc",
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 123,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "Gullberg was suddenly a big player: he was welcomed with respect in the exclusive club of selected warriors, all known to one another, where the contacts were made through personal friendship and trust, not through official channels and bureaucratic regulations. He met James Jesus Angleton, and he got to drink whisky at a discreet club in London with the chief of MI6. He was one of the elite.\nHe was never going to be able to tell anyone about his triumphs, not even in posthumous memoirs. And there was the ever-present anxiety that the Enemy would notice his overseas journeys, that he might attract attention, that he might involuntarily lead the Russians to Zalachenko. In that respect, Zalachenko was his worst enemy.",
            "paragraph_end": 124,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "d06a3589fb8a8f97e84f8c6031b7c0ab889bf072a11633113e4c8bcf624bbbb7",
            "id": "d06a3589-fb8a-8f97-e84f-8c6031b7c0ab",
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 128,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "Everything could have gone fine. By the early eighties Zalachenko had calmed down and begun to adapt. But he never gave up the whore Salander—and worse, he had become the father of Camilla and Lisbeth Salander.\nLisbeth Salander .\nGullberg pronounced the name with displeasure.\nEver since the girls were nine or ten, he had had a bad feeling about Lisbeth. He did not need a psychiatrist to tell him that she was not normal. Björck had reported that she was vicious and aggressive towards her father and that she seemed to be not in the least afraid of him. She did not say much, but she expressed her dissatisfaction in a thousand other ways. She was a problem in the making, but how gigantic this problem would become was something Gullberg could never have imagined in his wildest dreams. What he most feared was that the situation in the Salander family would give rise to a social welfare report that named Zalachenko. Time and again he urged the man to cut his ties and disappear from their lives. Zalachenko would give his word, and then would always break it. He had other whores. He had plenty of whores. But after a few months he was always back with the Salander woman.",
            "paragraph_end": 131,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "72122f1e1d22e67e2ffdf7127a23bd7d1d4caf273f315a3204d7e38daf504f3e",
            "id": "72122f1e-1d22-e67e-2ffd-f7127a23bd7d",
            "agent_label": "E3"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 126,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "By that time it was already too late. Zalachenko had gone and fucked that stupid whore Agneta Sofia Salander, née Sjölander, and he had heedlessly told her his real name—Zalachenko. Gullberg began to believe that Zalachenko was not quite right in the head. He suspected that the Russian defector wanted to be exposed. It was as if he needed a platform. How else to explain the fact that he had been so fucking stupid?",
            "paragraph_end": 126,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "a22f4f5ba0e175c40ebcdfc0cf51e98c610c170267d6b6c2e18be06aa75b135b",
            "id": "a22f4f5b-a0e1-75c4-0ebc-dfc0cf51e98c",
            "agent_label": "E4"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 127,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "There were whores, there were periods of excessive drinking, and there were incidents of violence with bouncers and others. On three occasions Zalachenko was arrested by the Swedish police for drunkenness, and twice more in connection with fights in bars. Every time, the Section had to intervene discreetly and bail him out, seeing to it that documents disappeared and records were altered. Gullberg assigned Björck to babysit the defector almost around the clock. It was not an easy job, but there was no alternative.",
            "paragraph_end": 127,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "9a06654c0052d55df0a1aceda750b5aad12c0265b7aa9b96dec5ae1522528356",
            "id": "9a06654c-0052-d55d-f0a1-aceda750b5aa",
            "agent_label": "E5"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 125,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "During the first year, the defector had lived in an anonymous apartment owned by the Section. He did not exist in any registry or in any public document. Those within the Zalachenko unit thought they had plenty of time before they had to plan his future. Not until the spring of 1978 was he given a passport in the name of Karl Axel Bodin, along with a laboriously crafted personal history—a fictitious but verifiable background in Swedish records.",
            "paragraph_end": 125,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "57575632b714562f96841eefc2e2f1a9ec91fd99e1af4b49c9fabe566223526d",
            "id": "57575632-b714-562f-9684-1eefc2e2f1a9",
            "agent_label": "E6"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_009.html",
            "paragraph": 102,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 2 Friday, December 20",
            "page": "8",
            "text": "“Just tell what you think is important.”\n“He comes off a little like Practical Pig in The Three Little Pigs . So far he has been an excellent journalist. In the eighties he had a lot of temporary jobs, first in the provincial press and then in Stockholm. There’s a list. His breakthrough came with the story about the Bear Gang—the bank robbers he identified.”\n“Kalle Blomkvist.”\n“He hates the nickname, which is understandable. Somebody’d get a fat lip if they ever called me Pippi Longstocking on a newspaper placard.”",
            "paragraph_end": 105,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "81326f319fa15e3f3bf842ec0cbf231b0a33e74c5b853f428cde8cbeaf4d2f65",
            "id": "81326f31-9fa1-5e3f-3bf8-42ec0cbf231b",
            "score": -6.1181440353393555,
            "agent_label": "E7"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c16_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 160,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 16 Friday, May 27–Tuesday, May 31",
            "page": null,
            "text": "“One was Evert Gullberg. . . . He was the top man.”\nJaneryd nodded.\n“How many times did you meet him?”\n“He was at every meeting except one. There were about ten meetings during the time Fälldin was prime minister.”\n“Where did you meet?”\n“In the lobby of some hotel. Usually the Sheraton. Once at the Amaranth on Kungsholmen and sometimes at the Continental pub.”\n“And who else was at the meetings?”\n“It was a long time ago; I don’t remember.”",
            "paragraph_end": 167,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
