          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 123,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "Gullberg was suddenly a big player: he was welcomed with respect in the exclusive club of selected warriors, all known to one another, where the contacts were made through personal friendship and trust, not through official channels and bureaucratic regulations. He met James Jesus Angleton, and he got to drink whisky at a discreet club in London with the chief of MI6. He was one of the elite.\nHe was never going to be able to tell anyone about his triumphs, not even in posthumous memoirs. And there was the ever-present anxiety that the Enemy would notice his overseas journeys, that he might attract attention, that he might involuntarily lead the Russians to Zalachenko. In that respect, Zalachenko was his worst enemy.",
            "paragraph_end": 124,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "d06a3589fb8a8f97e84f8c6031b7c0ab889bf072a11633113e4c8bcf624bbbb7",
            "id": "d06a3589-fb8a-8f97-e84f-8c6031b7c0ab",
            "agent_label": "E2"
